1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,829
Sweetheart, two weeks before Christmas,

2
00:00:02,829 --> 00:00:06,240
my daughter-in-law cleaned out my kitchen while

3
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:08,925
I was in the hospital with pneumonia,

4
00:00:08,925 --> 00:00:11,319
and she threw my mother's old tin

5
00:00:11,319 --> 00:00:13,061
recipe box in the trash.

6
00:00:13,241 --> 00:00:14,924
She said it was clutter.

7
00:00:15,104 --> 00:00:18,018
Greasy old cards nobody could read.

8
00:00:18,198 --> 00:00:20,389
What she didn't know was what my

9
00:00:20,389 --> 00:00:23,333
mother had hidden behind those recipe cards

10
00:00:23,333 --> 00:00:24,839
in nineteen sixty-one.

11
00:00:25,019 --> 00:00:26,737
And neither did I.

12
00:00:27,087 --> 00:00:29,187
Tonight I'm going to tell you how

13
00:00:29,187 --> 00:00:30,459
I got that box back,

14
00:00:30,459 --> 00:00:33,258
what my granddaughter found taped inside the

15
00:00:33,258 --> 00:00:34,593
lid on Christmas Eve,

16
00:00:34,593 --> 00:00:37,201
and the letter in my mother's handwriting

17
00:00:37,201 --> 00:00:39,491
that made my whole family cry around

18
00:00:39,491 --> 00:00:40,636
the kitchen table.

19
00:00:40,816 --> 00:00:41,867
Stay with me,

20
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:44,940
honey, because the last recipe card in

21
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:47,527
that box wasn't a recipe at all.

22
00:00:47,876 --> 00:00:50,250
My mother's recipe box was a red

23
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,846
tin box with a little painted Dutch

24
00:00:52,846 --> 00:00:54,032
girl on the lid,

25
00:00:54,032 --> 00:00:56,480
honey, worn down to bare metal at

26
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:59,150
the corners from sixty years of use.

27
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:02,383
Inside were about two hundred index cards

28
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:03,798
in her handwriting,

29
00:01:03,798 --> 00:01:05,064
and her mother's,

30
00:01:05,064 --> 00:01:06,331
and some in mine.

31
00:01:06,511 --> 00:01:09,599
Spattered with butter and vanilla and grease.

32
00:01:09,779 --> 00:01:11,068
Pot roast.

33
00:01:11,248 --> 00:01:12,478
Fudge.

34
00:01:12,658 --> 00:01:14,458
Molasses cookies.

35
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:16,217
Banana bread.

36
00:01:16,397 --> 00:01:17,639
Divinity.

37
00:01:17,819 --> 00:01:20,036
My mother's Christmas rolls.

38
00:01:20,216 --> 00:01:23,078
Every recipe I've ever shown you came

39
00:01:23,078 --> 00:01:24,315
out of that box.

40
00:01:24,664 --> 00:01:28,086
When my mother died in nineteen eighty-nine,

41
00:01:28,086 --> 00:01:30,342
that box was the only thing I

42
00:01:30,342 --> 00:01:31,120
asked for.

43
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,854
My brother Danny took the furniture.

44
00:01:34,034 --> 00:01:35,508
I took the box.

45
00:01:35,688 --> 00:01:37,861
It sat on my kitchen counter by

46
00:01:37,861 --> 00:01:40,103
the stove for thirty-five years,

47
00:01:40,103 --> 00:01:42,276
and I looked something up in it

48
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:43,467
almost every day.

49
00:01:43,647 --> 00:01:45,423
I never cleaned it out.

50
00:01:45,603 --> 00:01:47,536
I never went all the way to

51
00:01:47,536 --> 00:01:48,181
the back.

52
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:50,172
I didn't need to.

53
00:01:50,352 --> 00:01:52,267
In a minute I'll tell you what

54
00:01:52,267 --> 00:01:53,289
was in the back.

55
00:01:53,639 --> 00:01:56,463
My mother learned to cook from her

56
00:01:56,463 --> 00:01:58,290
mother, Grandma Pearl,

57
00:01:58,290 --> 00:02:00,781
on a farm with no electricity,

58
00:02:00,781 --> 00:02:01,279
honey.

59
00:02:01,459 --> 00:02:04,553
Grandma Pearl couldn't read very well,

60
00:02:04,553 --> 00:02:07,241
so she never wrote anything down.

61
00:02:07,421 --> 00:02:09,736
My mother would stand next to her

62
00:02:09,736 --> 00:02:11,841
at the wood stove and write it

63
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:13,806
all down on scraps of paper.

64
00:02:13,986 --> 00:02:15,809
A handful of this.

65
00:02:15,989 --> 00:02:17,405
A pinch of that.

66
00:02:17,585 --> 00:02:19,524
Butter the size of an egg.

67
00:02:19,704 --> 00:02:22,393
Then she'd copy it onto cards at

68
00:02:22,393 --> 00:02:22,897
night.

69
00:02:23,077 --> 00:02:25,062
That's how the box started.

70
00:02:25,412 --> 00:02:27,762
Some of those cards had more than

71
00:02:27,762 --> 00:02:28,902
recipes on them,

72
00:02:28,902 --> 00:02:29,685
sweetheart.

73
00:02:29,865 --> 00:02:32,840
On the back of the fudge card,

74
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:34,426
my mother wrote,

75
00:02:34,426 --> 00:02:36,410
Ruthie's first time,

76
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:37,798
she burned it,

77
00:02:37,798 --> 00:02:38,294
1951.

78
00:02:38,474 --> 00:02:40,303
On the pot roast card,

79
00:02:40,303 --> 00:02:44,128
Walter's favorite, never use the cheap onions.

80
00:02:44,308 --> 00:02:46,607
On the molasses cookie card,

81
00:02:46,607 --> 00:02:48,084
in my handwriting,

82
00:02:48,084 --> 00:02:49,973
from when I was twelve,

83
00:02:49,973 --> 00:02:51,204
too much clove!

84
00:02:51,384 --> 00:02:53,753
Every card was a little diary.

85
00:02:53,933 --> 00:02:56,603
Our whole family was in that box.

86
00:02:56,952 --> 00:03:00,236
In December of two thousand twenty-three,

87
00:03:00,236 --> 00:03:01,517
I got pneumonia,

88
00:03:01,517 --> 00:03:02,398
sweetheart.

89
00:03:02,578 --> 00:03:05,225
Bad pneumonia, at seventy-nine.

90
00:03:05,405 --> 00:03:07,764
I was in the hospital for eleven

91
00:03:07,764 --> 00:03:08,133
days.

92
00:03:08,313 --> 00:03:10,378
And while I was there,

93
00:03:10,378 --> 00:03:11,691
my son's wife,

94
00:03:11,691 --> 00:03:13,944
Denise, decided to help.

95
00:03:14,124 --> 00:03:16,227
She went to my house with a

96
00:03:16,227 --> 00:03:17,630
box of trash bags,

97
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:20,045
and she cleaned out my kitchen.

98
00:03:20,225 --> 00:03:21,361
She meant well,

99
00:03:21,361 --> 00:03:21,815
honey.

100
00:03:21,995 --> 00:03:23,621
I want to say that.

101
00:03:23,801 --> 00:03:25,345
But she didn't ask.

102
00:03:25,525 --> 00:03:27,770
She wanted me to come home to

103
00:03:27,770 --> 00:03:29,627
a clean, modern kitchen,

104
00:03:29,627 --> 00:03:31,794
like the ones on television.

105
00:03:32,144 --> 00:03:34,668
She threw away my old spice tins,

106
00:03:34,668 --> 00:03:36,504
my chipped mixing bowls,

107
00:03:36,504 --> 00:03:39,563
my mother's rolling pin with the cracked

108
00:03:39,563 --> 00:03:42,317
handle, and a drawer of rubber bands

109
00:03:42,317 --> 00:03:43,464
and twist ties.

110
00:03:43,644 --> 00:03:46,779
And she threw away the recipe box.

111
00:03:46,959 --> 00:03:48,198
She said later,

112
00:03:48,198 --> 00:03:50,593
Mom, those cards were filthy.

113
00:03:50,773 --> 00:03:53,025
You can't even read half of them.

114
00:03:53,205 --> 00:03:55,288
I'll type up the good ones for

115
00:03:55,288 --> 00:03:56,677
you on the computer.

116
00:03:56,857 --> 00:03:58,721
And she put it in a black

117
00:03:58,721 --> 00:04:01,257
trash bag on the curb for Thursday

118
00:04:01,257 --> 00:04:01,779
pickup.

119
00:04:02,129 --> 00:04:05,018
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

120
00:04:05,018 --> 00:04:07,247
tap the like button for me,

121
00:04:07,247 --> 00:04:08,155
sweetheart.

122
00:04:08,335 --> 00:04:11,365
It helps more people find these stories.

123
00:04:11,545 --> 00:04:14,436
I came home on a Wednesday afternoon.

124
00:04:14,616 --> 00:04:16,596
I walked into my kitchen,

125
00:04:16,596 --> 00:04:18,575
and I knew in one second.

126
00:04:18,755 --> 00:04:20,949
The empty spot on the counter by

127
00:04:20,949 --> 00:04:21,634
the stove.

128
00:04:21,814 --> 00:04:24,813
I asked Denise where my mother's box

129
00:04:24,813 --> 00:04:25,146
was.

130
00:04:25,326 --> 00:04:26,622
And she said,

131
00:04:26,622 --> 00:04:28,217
oh, I tossed it.

132
00:04:28,397 --> 00:04:30,534
It's in the bags on the curb.

133
00:04:30,714 --> 00:04:32,502
Pickup's tomorrow.

134
00:04:32,851 --> 00:04:34,928
I went out to that curb in

135
00:04:34,928 --> 00:04:36,925
my bathrobe and slippers,

136
00:04:36,925 --> 00:04:38,443
honey, in the snow,

137
00:04:38,443 --> 00:04:41,558
seventy-nine years old and two days out

138
00:04:41,558 --> 00:04:42,836
of the hospital.

139
00:04:43,016 --> 00:04:45,983
And I started tearing open those trash

140
00:04:45,983 --> 00:04:47,857
bags with my bare hands.

141
00:04:48,037 --> 00:04:50,638
Denise ran out after me yelling,

142
00:04:50,638 --> 00:04:53,401
Mom, Mom, you're going to get sick

143
00:04:53,401 --> 00:04:53,889
again!

144
00:04:54,069 --> 00:04:55,822
And my son came out,

145
00:04:55,822 --> 00:04:58,100
and my granddaughter Lily,

146
00:04:58,100 --> 00:04:59,502
who was sixteen.

147
00:04:59,682 --> 00:05:02,132
And they all stood there watching me.

148
00:05:02,481 --> 00:05:04,316
It was in the fourth bag,

149
00:05:04,316 --> 00:05:06,150
under the coffee grounds.

150
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:07,809
The lid was dented,

151
00:05:07,809 --> 00:05:10,145
and some of the cards were wet

152
00:05:10,145 --> 00:05:11,079
and stained.

153
00:05:11,259 --> 00:05:13,439
I sat right down on the curb

154
00:05:13,439 --> 00:05:15,854
in the snow and held it against

155
00:05:15,854 --> 00:05:17,333
my chest and cried,

156
00:05:17,333 --> 00:05:18,190
sweetheart.

157
00:05:18,370 --> 00:05:20,390
Not because of the cards.

158
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:22,044
Because of my mother.

159
00:05:22,224 --> 00:05:24,296
That box was the last thing in

160
00:05:24,296 --> 00:05:27,058
the world that still had her handwriting

161
00:05:27,058 --> 00:05:27,472
in it.

162
00:05:27,822 --> 00:05:30,069
Lily sat down next to me on

163
00:05:30,069 --> 00:05:30,818
the curb.

164
00:05:30,998 --> 00:05:33,041
She put her arm around me.

165
00:05:33,221 --> 00:05:34,361
And she said,

166
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:37,343
Grandma, let me help you dry them.

167
00:05:37,523 --> 00:05:39,125
Every single one.

168
00:05:39,305 --> 00:05:42,133
And Denise stood there on the sidewalk,

169
00:05:42,133 --> 00:05:43,728
and her face crumpled.

170
00:05:43,908 --> 00:05:45,341
She said, Mom,

171
00:05:45,341 --> 00:05:46,672
I'm so sorry.

172
00:05:46,852 --> 00:05:48,303
I didn't know.

173
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:51,559
I thought they were just old recipes.

174
00:05:51,739 --> 00:05:52,567
And I said,

175
00:05:52,567 --> 00:05:55,200
honey, nothing in a kitchen is just

176
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:55,501
old.

177
00:05:55,681 --> 00:05:58,026
That's the whole point of a kitchen.

178
00:05:58,377 --> 00:06:00,119
And here's the thing,

179
00:06:00,119 --> 00:06:00,617
honey.

180
00:06:00,797 --> 00:06:03,073
I wasn't angry at Denise.

181
00:06:03,253 --> 00:06:04,402
Not really.

182
00:06:04,582 --> 00:06:06,914
She came from a family where nobody

183
00:06:06,914 --> 00:06:07,380
cooked.

184
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:10,053
Her mother worked two jobs and they

185
00:06:10,053 --> 00:06:11,763
ate from cans and boxes.

186
00:06:11,943 --> 00:06:14,316
She didn't know what a recipe box

187
00:06:14,316 --> 00:06:17,193
meant, because nobody had ever given her

188
00:06:17,193 --> 00:06:17,481
one.

189
00:06:17,661 --> 00:06:20,343
You can't miss what you've never had.

190
00:06:20,523 --> 00:06:22,970
That night, I decided I was going

191
00:06:22,970 --> 00:06:24,157
to give her one.

192
00:06:24,507 --> 00:06:26,335
So far, the hospital,

193
00:06:26,335 --> 00:06:28,860
the curb, and the fourth bag.

194
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,870
That night, Lily and I spread every

195
00:06:31,870 --> 00:06:34,619
one of those two hundred cards out

196
00:06:34,619 --> 00:06:36,559
on my dining room table,

197
00:06:36,559 --> 00:06:38,015
on towels, to dry.

198
00:06:38,195 --> 00:06:39,391
One by one.

199
00:06:39,571 --> 00:06:41,233
And as we laid them out,

200
00:06:41,233 --> 00:06:43,450
I told her the story behind each

201
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:43,727
one.

202
00:06:43,907 --> 00:06:46,518
The fudge my mother made the Christmas

203
00:06:46,518 --> 00:06:48,098
my father lost his job.

204
00:06:48,278 --> 00:06:50,750
The pot roast for every Sunday for

205
00:06:50,750 --> 00:06:51,622
forty years.

206
00:06:51,972 --> 00:06:54,208
And then, at the very back of

207
00:06:54,208 --> 00:06:56,522
the box, behind the last card,

208
00:06:56,522 --> 00:06:59,453
Lily found something I'd never seen in

209
00:06:59,453 --> 00:07:00,842
thirty-five years.

210
00:07:01,022 --> 00:07:03,688
The cardboard lining of the box was

211
00:07:03,688 --> 00:07:04,145
loose.

212
00:07:04,325 --> 00:07:05,401
And behind it,

213
00:07:05,401 --> 00:07:08,167
taped flat against the bottom of the

214
00:07:08,167 --> 00:07:09,781
tin, was an envelope.

215
00:07:09,961 --> 00:07:11,703
Yellowed and brittle.

216
00:07:11,883 --> 00:07:13,218
And on the front,

217
00:07:13,218 --> 00:07:15,966
in my mother's careful handwriting,

218
00:07:15,966 --> 00:07:17,537
it said, For Ruthie.

219
00:07:17,717 --> 00:07:19,714
Open when you need it most.

220
00:07:20,064 --> 00:07:22,289
My hands shook so hard Lily had

221
00:07:22,289 --> 00:07:23,581
to open it for me.

222
00:07:23,761 --> 00:07:25,990
Inside were three things,

223
00:07:25,990 --> 00:07:26,525
honey.

224
00:07:26,705 --> 00:07:27,826
A savings bond,

225
00:07:27,826 --> 00:07:29,620
from nineteen sixty-one,

226
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:32,161
for two hundred and fifty dollars.

227
00:07:32,341 --> 00:07:34,547
That was a fortune for my mother.

228
00:07:34,727 --> 00:07:37,167
And a little lock of fine blonde

229
00:07:37,167 --> 00:07:39,836
baby hair, tied with a pink thread.

230
00:07:40,016 --> 00:07:40,851
Mine.

231
00:07:41,031 --> 00:07:42,181
And a letter.

232
00:07:42,361 --> 00:07:44,687
Two pages, in her handwriting,

233
00:07:44,687 --> 00:07:46,936
dated December the twentieth,

234
00:07:46,936 --> 00:07:48,410
nineteen sixty-one.

235
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:50,866
Lily read it out loud to me,

236
00:07:50,866 --> 00:07:53,574
sweetheart, at my dining room table,

237
00:07:53,574 --> 00:07:56,283
with the cards drying all around us.

238
00:07:56,463 --> 00:07:57,607
My mother wrote,

239
00:07:57,607 --> 00:07:59,396
Ruthie, you're seventeen,

240
00:07:59,396 --> 00:08:01,685
and you think I'm old-fashioned,

241
00:08:01,685 --> 00:08:03,545
and you're probably right.

242
00:08:03,725 --> 00:08:06,086
I've saved this bond a dollar at

243
00:08:06,086 --> 00:08:08,005
a time, from my egg money,

244
00:08:08,005 --> 00:08:09,112
for five years.

245
00:08:09,292 --> 00:08:10,987
Your father doesn't know.

246
00:08:11,167 --> 00:08:12,514
It's for you.

247
00:08:12,694 --> 00:08:14,911
Not for a dress or a car.

248
00:08:15,091 --> 00:08:17,079
For a day when you're scared and

249
00:08:17,079 --> 00:08:19,004
alone and you need your mother.

250
00:08:19,354 --> 00:08:20,734
And then she wrote,

251
00:08:20,734 --> 00:08:22,988
I'm hiding it in my recipe box,

252
00:08:22,988 --> 00:08:25,822
because you'll find it when you finally

253
00:08:25,822 --> 00:08:28,293
start cooking for your own family.

254
00:08:28,473 --> 00:08:30,823
Every recipe in here is a way

255
00:08:30,823 --> 00:08:32,525
of saying I love you.

256
00:08:32,705 --> 00:08:34,754
When you make my fudge for your

257
00:08:34,754 --> 00:08:37,001
children, you're saying it for me.

258
00:08:37,181 --> 00:08:38,316
When I'm gone,

259
00:08:38,316 --> 00:08:40,748
I'll still be in your kitchen,

260
00:08:40,748 --> 00:08:42,127
every single day.

261
00:08:42,307 --> 00:08:43,990
Love, Mama.

262
00:08:44,340 --> 00:08:46,305
There was a postscript,

263
00:08:46,305 --> 00:08:47,672
too, sweetheart.

264
00:08:47,852 --> 00:08:50,567
At the bottom of the second page,

265
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:52,377
my mother had written,

266
00:08:52,377 --> 00:08:52,542
P.

267
00:08:52,722 --> 00:08:53,546
S.

268
00:08:53,726 --> 00:08:55,322
If you're reading this,

269
00:08:55,322 --> 00:08:58,165
you've finally cleaned out my recipe box,

270
00:08:58,165 --> 00:09:01,217
and you're probably standing in your kitchen

271
00:09:01,217 --> 00:09:01,702
crying.

272
00:09:01,882 --> 00:09:03,357
Go make the fudge.

273
00:09:03,537 --> 00:09:05,336
And then call your brother.

274
00:09:05,516 --> 00:09:07,270
You two fight too much.

275
00:09:07,450 --> 00:09:09,538
Lily laughed so hard she had to

276
00:09:09,538 --> 00:09:10,886
put the letter down.

277
00:09:11,066 --> 00:09:13,753
Because Danny and I hadn't spoken in

278
00:09:13,753 --> 00:09:14,723
three months.

279
00:09:15,074 --> 00:09:17,555
So that's exactly what I did,

280
00:09:17,555 --> 00:09:18,069
honey.

281
00:09:18,249 --> 00:09:20,093
At ten o'clock at night,

282
00:09:20,093 --> 00:09:22,322
two days out of the hospital,

283
00:09:22,322 --> 00:09:24,243
I made my mother's fudge,

284
00:09:24,243 --> 00:09:25,703
with Lily stirring.

285
00:09:25,883 --> 00:09:28,415
And then I called my brother Danny,

286
00:09:28,415 --> 00:09:30,875
in Florida, seventy-six years old.

287
00:09:31,055 --> 00:09:33,048
I read him the letter over the

288
00:09:33,048 --> 00:09:33,446
phone.

289
00:09:33,626 --> 00:09:35,806
And my little brother cried,

290
00:09:35,806 --> 00:09:36,740
and I cried,

291
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:39,076
and we made up whatever it was

292
00:09:39,076 --> 00:09:41,022
we'd been fighting about.

293
00:09:41,202 --> 00:09:43,501
I can't even remember now.

294
00:09:43,851 --> 00:09:46,789
She'd hidden it sixty-two years before,

295
00:09:46,789 --> 00:09:47,241
honey.

296
00:09:47,421 --> 00:09:48,988
And I never found it.

297
00:09:49,168 --> 00:09:51,220
Because I never went all the way

298
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:51,989
to the back.

299
00:09:52,169 --> 00:09:54,591
I'd been cooking from that box every

300
00:09:54,591 --> 00:09:56,341
day for thirty-five years,

301
00:09:56,341 --> 00:09:58,695
and she'd been right there under my

302
00:09:58,695 --> 00:10:00,041
hand the whole time.

303
00:10:00,221 --> 00:10:02,601
Waiting for the day I needed her

304
00:10:02,601 --> 00:10:02,972
most.

305
00:10:03,152 --> 00:10:05,117
And that day turned out to be

306
00:10:05,117 --> 00:10:07,082
two days out of the hospital,

307
00:10:07,082 --> 00:10:09,520
at seventy-nine, crying on a curb in

308
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,130
the snow.

309
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:13,053
The savings bond had grown,

310
00:10:13,053 --> 00:10:14,578
too, sweetheart.

311
00:10:14,758 --> 00:10:18,021
Those old bonds stopped earning interest after

312
00:10:18,021 --> 00:10:20,362
forty years, but when my son took

313
00:10:20,362 --> 00:10:21,426
it to the bank,

314
00:10:21,426 --> 00:10:23,838
it was still worth over a thousand

315
00:10:23,838 --> 00:10:24,406
dollars.

316
00:10:24,586 --> 00:10:26,842
And I knew exactly what to do

317
00:10:26,842 --> 00:10:27,465
with it.

318
00:10:27,645 --> 00:10:28,979
I gave it to Lily,

319
00:10:28,979 --> 00:10:31,127
who had sat on that curb with

320
00:10:31,127 --> 00:10:32,312
me, for college.

321
00:10:32,492 --> 00:10:34,489
She's studying to be a nurse.

322
00:10:34,669 --> 00:10:36,403
She said, Grandma,

323
00:10:36,403 --> 00:10:38,233
it's the egg money.

324
00:10:38,413 --> 00:10:39,667
I'll make it count.

325
00:10:40,018 --> 00:10:41,713
And Denise, honey?

326
00:10:41,893 --> 00:10:43,252
On Christmas Eve,

327
00:10:43,252 --> 00:10:45,410
she came to my house with a

328
00:10:45,410 --> 00:10:46,769
big flat package.

329
00:10:46,949 --> 00:10:48,407
Inside was a frame,

330
00:10:48,407 --> 00:10:49,174
and in it,

331
00:10:49,174 --> 00:10:52,244
behind glass, was my mother's letter and

332
00:10:52,244 --> 00:10:53,548
the lock of hair,

333
00:10:53,548 --> 00:10:56,388
preserved so they'd never fall apart.

334
00:10:56,568 --> 00:10:57,785
And she said,

335
00:10:57,785 --> 00:10:59,377
Mom, I was wrong.

336
00:10:59,557 --> 00:11:01,752
I want to learn the recipes.

337
00:11:01,932 --> 00:11:03,104
All of them.

338
00:11:03,284 --> 00:11:04,828
In your kitchen.

339
00:11:05,008 --> 00:11:06,552
In her handwriting.

340
00:11:06,732 --> 00:11:08,149
Will you teach me?

341
00:11:08,499 --> 00:11:10,159
Every Saturday since,

342
00:11:10,159 --> 00:11:12,293
Denise comes to my kitchen,

343
00:11:12,293 --> 00:11:14,823
and we make one recipe from that

344
00:11:14,823 --> 00:11:15,139
box.

345
00:11:15,319 --> 00:11:17,549
We're up to card ninety-four.

346
00:11:17,729 --> 00:11:19,167
She's a good cook,

347
00:11:19,167 --> 00:11:22,523
sweetheart, better than me with pie crust.

348
00:11:22,703 --> 00:11:23,850
And last month,

349
00:11:23,850 --> 00:11:26,603
she taped an envelope inside the lid

350
00:11:26,603 --> 00:11:28,590
of her own new recipe box,

351
00:11:28,590 --> 00:11:29,890
for her daughter.

352
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:32,485
She won't tell anybody what's in it.

353
00:11:32,665 --> 00:11:34,546
That's how it should be.

354
00:11:34,896 --> 00:11:37,671
Here's what I want you to remember,

355
00:11:37,671 --> 00:11:38,146
honey.

356
00:11:38,326 --> 00:11:40,636
The old things in your kitchen aren't

357
00:11:40,636 --> 00:11:41,136
clutter.

358
00:11:41,316 --> 00:11:44,130
They're letters from people who loved you,

359
00:11:44,130 --> 00:11:46,006
written in butter and flour.

360
00:11:46,186 --> 00:11:49,361
Before you throw anything away this Christmas,

361
00:11:49,361 --> 00:11:51,225
go all the way to the back.

362
00:11:51,405 --> 00:11:53,194
Tell me in the comments,

363
00:11:53,194 --> 00:11:56,174
sweetheart, do you have your mother's or

364
00:11:56,174 --> 00:11:57,814
grandmother's recipes?

365
00:11:57,994 --> 00:12:00,218
There are more of my family's stories

366
00:12:00,218 --> 00:12:02,322
in the playlist right there on your

367
00:12:02,322 --> 00:12:02,742
screen.

368
00:12:02,922 --> 00:12:03,747
Good night.
